---
import ProjectLayout from '../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro';
import RiderCompare from '../../components/visuals/RiderCompare.astro';
import '../../styles/details.css';
---
<ProjectLayout
  title="Is the Algorithm Being Fair to Riders?"
  description="Analysing 15,000 delivery records to separate rider skill from circumstance, and building a fairer score."
  question="Can a performance score be mathematically accurate and still be unfair?"
  intro="I analysed 15,000 delivery records to understand how much rider performance was being shaped by skill versus circumstances like traffic, weather, and route conditions."
  did={[
    { title: 'Reverse-engineered the scoring logic', body: 'Worked backwards from the existing score to understand what was driving it.' },
    { title: 'Separated skill from circumstance', body: 'Tested which factors reflected rider performance and which were largely outside the rider’s control.' },
    { title: 'Built a fairer alternative', body: 'Designed an adjusted scoring approach that reduced penalties for circumstances riders could not influence.' },
  ]}
  stoodOut="Riders with similar underlying performance could still receive meaningfully different scores because of the conditions around them."
  keyLine="A model can be consistent without necessarily being fair."
  proof={['15,000 records', '99.9% reconstructed fit', 'Fair Score framework']}
  reflection="This made me more interested in the difference between predicting performance and judging it fairly."
  detailsLabel="How I tested it"
  next={{ href: '/projects/healthcare-expenditure/', label: 'Next: Healthcare Expenditure' }}
>
  <RiderCompare slot="visual" />

  <div slot="details" class="xd-grid">
    <div class="xd-block">
      <h3>Score reconstruction</h3>
      <p>Treated the existing performance score as the thing to explain, and worked backwards to find which recorded factors reproduced it.</p>
      <p>The reconstructed score matched the original with a 99.9% fit, which meant the logic was understood well enough to question it.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="xd-block">
      <h3>Regression</h3>
      <p>Used regression to estimate how much each factor moved the score while holding the others constant.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="xd-block">
      <h3>Feature effects</h3>
      <p>Compared the size of each factor’s effect to see which ones were doing most of the work in the final number.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="xd-block">
      <h3>Controllable vs uncontrollable</h3>
      <p>Sorted the factors by whether a rider could reasonably influence them.</p>
      <div class="xd-split">
        <div><span class="lbl">largely outside the rider’s control</span>traffic · weather · route conditions</div>
        <div><span class="lbl">closer to rider performance</span>what remains once circumstance is accounted for</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="xd-block">
      <h3>Limitations</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Where a factor sits between “controllable” and “not” is partly a judgement call.</li>
        <li>The records describe the conditions that were captured, not everything that happens on a delivery.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</ProjectLayout>
